  <body>
    <div class="page">
      <header>
        <div class="container nav">
          <a class="brand" href="/">Feranzeey</a>
          <a class="back" href="/blog">← Back to Blog</a>
        </div>
      </header>

      <main>
        <section class="article-header">
          <div class="container">
            <div class="eyebrow">Platform Engineering · Technical Article</div>

            <h1>{title}</h1>

            <p class="description">{description}</p>

            <div class="meta">
              Published {date} · Feranzeey
            </div>

            <div class="tags">
              {tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <article>
          <div class="container">

            <p>
              Kubernetes makes it possible to run applications reliably at
              scale, but operating Kubernetes environments still requires
              engineers to investigate multiple sources of information when
              something goes wrong.
            </p>

            <p>
              A deployment might become unavailable. A pod might enter
              <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>. A readiness probe might fail. At
              that point, an engineer may need to check Kubernetes resources,
              events, metrics, recent deployments and application changes
              before understanding what actually happened.
            </p>

            <p>
              I wanted to explore how these operational signals could be
              brought together into a single platform.
            </p>

            <p>
              That led me to build <strong>PlatformOps AI</strong> — an
              AI-powered Kubernetes operations platform designed to combine
              Kubernetes resources, Prometheus metrics and GitHub activity with
              automated investigation and operational insights.
            </p>

            <p>
              The goal was not simply to build another Kubernetes dashboard.
            </p>

            <p>
              The goal was to make incident investigation more structured and
              easier to understand.
            </p>

            <h2>The Problem</h2>

            <p>
              When troubleshooting a Kubernetes incident, information is often
              distributed across several tools.
            </p>

            <p>An engineer may move between:</p>

            <pre><code>kubectl
   ↓
Kubernetes Events
   ↓
Prometheus
   ↓
Grafana
   ↓
GitHub
   ↓
Application logs</code></pre>

            <p>
              Each tool provides useful information, but the engineer still
              has to connect the pieces.
            </p>

            <p>
              For example, if a deployment suddenly becomes unhealthy, I want
              to be able to answer:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>What is currently failing?</li>
              <li>Which Kubernetes resource is affected?</li>
              <li>What happened before the failure?</li>
              <li>Are there abnormal resource metrics?</li>
              <li>Was there a recent code or deployment change?</li>
              <li>What could be causing the issue?</li>
              <li>What action should be considered?</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              These questions became the foundation for PlatformOps AI.
            </p>

            <h2>What I Built</h2>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI is a Flask-based platform that brings several
              operational signals into one interface.
            </p>

            <p>The platform integrates with:</p>

            <ul>
              <li>Kubernetes</li>
              <li>Prometheus</li>
              <li>GitHub</li>
              <li>AI-assisted investigation</li>
              <li>Incident history</li>
              <li>Remediation workflows</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              The idea is to move from simply
              <strong>observing infrastructure</strong> toward
              <strong>assisting with infrastructure investigation</strong>.
            </p>

            <pre class="diagram"><code>                    Platform Engineer
                           │
                           ▼
                  PlatformOps AI
                     Dashboard
                           │
          ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
          │                │                │
          ▼                ▼                ▼
     Kubernetes        Prometheus        GitHub
      Resources          Metrics         Activity
          │                │                │
          └────────────────┼────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
                  Investigation Engine
                           │
              ┌────────────┼────────────┐
              │            │            │
              ▼            ▼            ▼
          Evidence     Root Cause    Severity
          Analysis      Analysis     Assessment
              │            │            │
              └────────────┼────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
                    Incident Report
                           │
                           ▼
                     Remediation</code></pre>

            <p>
              The architecture allows the platform to collect infrastructure
              information before producing a structured operational view of an
              incident.
            </p>

            <h2>Kubernetes Integration</h2>

            <p>
              Kubernetes is at the center of the platform.
            </p>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI interacts with Kubernetes to collect information
              about resources running inside the cluster.
            </p>

            <p>The platform works with resources such as:</p>

            <pre><code>Pods
Deployments
Services
Nodes
Namespaces
Events</code></pre>

            <p>
              This information provides the infrastructure context required
              for investigation.
            </p>

            <p>For example, instead of only seeing:</p>

            <pre><code>Deployment: grafana
Status: Unavailable</code></pre>

            <p>
              the platform can investigate the surrounding Kubernetes state.
            </p>

            <p>
              This is important because the status of one resource rarely
              tells the entire story.
            </p>

            <p>
              A deployment might be unavailable because:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Pods are failing</li>
              <li>Containers are repeatedly restarting</li>
              <li>Images cannot be pulled</li>
              <li>Readiness probes are failing</li>
              <li>Resources are constrained</li>
              <li>Another Kubernetes event occurred before the failure</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              The Kubernetes integration therefore provides the infrastructure
              evidence needed for deeper investigation.
            </p>

            <h2>Adding Prometheus Observability</h2>

            <p>
              Infrastructure state alone is not always enough.
            </p>

            <p>
              I also integrated Prometheus so PlatformOps AI could work with
              operational metrics.
            </p>

            <p>
              The platform can retrieve information around areas such as:
            </p>

            <pre><code>CPU utilization
Memory utilization
Network activity
Cluster utilization</code></pre>

            <p>
              This adds another layer of context.
            </p>

            <p>For example:</p>

            <pre><code>Kubernetes:
Pod is unhealthy

+

Prometheus:
CPU utilization has increased significantly

+

GitHub:
A recent deployment occurred

↓

Investigation:
Correlate the available evidence</code></pre>

            <p>
              This doesn't mean that every metric spike automatically
              represents the root cause.
            </p>

            <p>
              Instead, the metrics become another source of evidence that can
              be considered during investigation.
            </p>

            <p>
              That distinction is important when designing operational
              tooling.
            </p>

            <h2>GitHub Integration</h2>

            <p>
              Another part of the platform is GitHub integration.
            </p>

            <p>
              Infrastructure problems can sometimes appear shortly after
              application changes or deployments.
            </p>

            <p>
              Therefore, recent repository activity can provide useful context
              during an investigation.
            </p>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI can retrieve information such as:
            </p>

            <pre><code>Latest commit
Commit author
Commit date
Commit message</code></pre>

            <p>
              This creates a relationship between infrastructure state and
              recent development activity.
            </p>

            <p>For example:</p>

            <pre><code>Application deployment
        ↓
New commit
        ↓
Deployment becomes unhealthy
        ↓
Kubernetes Events
        ↓
Prometheus metrics
        ↓
Investigation</code></pre>

            <p>
              This is useful because incident investigation is often about
              understanding <strong>what changed</strong> as well as
              <strong>what is currently broken</strong>.
            </p>

            <h2>AI-Assisted Investigation</h2>

            <p>
              The most interesting part of the project is the investigation
              workflow.
            </p>

            <p>
              Rather than presenting raw infrastructure information and leaving
              the engineer to interpret everything manually, PlatformOps AI
              organizes the available evidence into a structured investigation.
            </p>

            <p>The investigation can identify situations such as:</p>

            <pre><code>CrashLoopBackOff
ImagePullBackOff
Failed Deployments
Unhealthy Pods
Readiness Probe Failures
Resource Problems</code></pre>

            <p>
              The resulting investigation can be structured around:
            </p>

            <pre><code>Issue
Evidence
Root Cause
Severity
Business Impact
Recommended Action</code></pre>

            <p>
              The purpose is to make the investigation easier to understand
              and act on.
            </p>

            <p>For example:</p>

            <pre><code>Issue:
Grafana deployment unavailable

Evidence:
Ready replicas: 0/1

Impact:
Monitoring service unavailable

Severity:
HIGH

Recommended action:
Investigate the failed deployment and
consider a rollout restart if appropriate.</code></pre>

            <p>
              The important part here is that the AI is assisting the
              investigation rather than replacing the underlying
              infrastructure evidence.
            </p>

            <p>
              The Kubernetes state, metrics and deployment information remain
              important parts of the investigation.
            </p>

            <h2>From Monitoring to Investigation</h2>

            <p>
              One of the ideas I wanted to explore with this project was the
              difference between <strong>monitoring</strong> and
              <strong>investigation</strong>.
            </p>

            <p>
              Monitoring answers:
            </p>

            <blockquote>
              <strong>Is something wrong?</strong>
            </blockquote>

            <p>
              Investigation asks:
            </p>

            <blockquote>
              <strong>
                What happened, what evidence do we have, and what should we
                investigate next?
              </strong>
            </blockquote>

            <p>For example:</p>

            <pre><code>Monitoring

Deployment unavailable
        ↓
Alert


Investigation

Deployment unavailable
        ↓
Check Pods
        ↓
Check Events
        ↓
Check Metrics
        ↓
Check Recent GitHub Activity
        ↓
Analyze Evidence
        ↓
Determine Possible Cause
        ↓
Recommend Action</code></pre>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI was designed around this second workflow.
            </p>

            <h2>Incident Timeline</h2>

            <p>
              Another important part of the platform is keeping track of
              investigations.
            </p>

            <p>
              An incident shouldn't simply disappear after the service becomes
              healthy again.
            </p>

            <p>
              The investigation itself can become operational knowledge.
            </p>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI records information such as:
            </p>

            <pre><code>Timestamp
Root Cause
Status
Repair Action
Resolution History</code></pre>

            <p>
              This creates an incident timeline that can help engineers
              understand what happened during an operational event.
            </p>

            <p>
              Over time, this type of history can become useful when
              investigating recurring problems.
            </p>

            <h2>Remediation</h2>

            <p>
              After investigation, the next question is usually:
            </p>

            <blockquote>
              <strong>What should I do about it?</strong>
            </blockquote>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI can provide recommended Kubernetes commands or
              remediation actions based on the investigation.
            </p>

            <p>For example:</p>

            <pre><code>kubectl rollout restart deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
              The important design principle is that recommendations should be
              connected to the evidence collected during the investigation.
            </p>

            <p>
              The system should not simply generate commands.
            </p>

            <p>
              It should provide context around <strong>why</strong> an action
              may be relevant.
            </p>

            <h2>Technology Stack</h2>

            <p>
              The project brought several DevOps and cloud-native technologies
              together.
            </p>

            <pre><code>Python
Flask
Kubernetes
Docker
Terraform
Prometheus
GitHub API
AI</code></pre>

            <p>Each technology has a specific role.</p>

            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Technology</th>
                  <th>Role</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>

              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Python</td>
                  <td>Application and automation logic</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Flask</td>
                  <td>Web application/API layer</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Kubernetes</td>
                  <td>Container orchestration environment</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Docker</td>
                  <td>Application containerization</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Terraform</td>
                  <td>Infrastructure as Code</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Prometheus</td>
                  <td>Metrics and observability</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>GitHub API</td>
                  <td>Repository and deployment activity</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>AI</td>
                  <td>Investigation and operational analysis</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>
              The combination made the project particularly interesting
              because it sits at the intersection of
              <strong>DevOps, platform engineering, observability and
              AI-assisted operations</strong>.
            </p>

            <h2>Infrastructure as Code</h2>

            <p>
              I also used Terraform as part of the infrastructure setup.
            </p>

            <p>
              This was important because I wanted infrastructure configuration
              to be reproducible rather than relying entirely on manual
              configuration.
            </p>

            <p>The general idea is:</p>

            <pre><code>Terraform
    ↓
Infrastructure
    ↓
Kubernetes
    ↓
PlatformOps AI
    ↓
Observability</code></pre>

            <p>
              Infrastructure as Code also makes it easier to reason about how
              the environment is created and maintained.
            </p>

            <h2>Challenges I Encountered</h2>

            <p>
              Building the project wasn't simply about connecting APIs
              together.
            </p>

            <p>
              One of the biggest challenges was thinking about how different
              infrastructure signals should be interpreted together.
            </p>

            <p>
              Kubernetes tells me about resource state.
            </p>

            <p>
              Prometheus tells me about metrics.
            </p>

            <p>
              GitHub tells me about recent development activity.
            </p>

            <p>
              AI can help structure and interpret the available information.
            </p>

            <p>
              But these signals don't automatically equal a root cause.
            </p>

            <div class="callout">
              <div class="callout-title">Key Lesson</div>

              <strong>Good incident automation depends on good evidence.</strong>

              <p style="margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The quality of the investigation depends heavily on the quality
                and relevance of the information being collected.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>What I Learned</h2>

            <p>
              Building PlatformOps AI strengthened my understanding of several
              areas of DevOps and platform engineering.
            </p>

            <h3>1. Observability is more than collecting metrics</h3>

            <p>
              Metrics are valuable, but their real operational value comes
              from being interpreted in context.
            </p>

            <h3>2. Kubernetes troubleshooting requires multiple signals</h3>

            <p>
              A Kubernetes resource status alone may not explain an incident.
            </p>

            <p>
              Events, metrics, deployment information and recent changes can
              provide additional context.
            </p>

            <h3>3. Automation should reduce investigation effort</h3>

            <p>
              The goal isn't to automate everything blindly.
            </p>

            <p>
              The goal is to remove repetitive work and help engineers reach
              useful information faster.
            </p>

            <h3>4. AI needs infrastructure context</h3>

            <p>
              An AI system working with infrastructure becomes more useful when
              it has access to relevant operational evidence.
            </p>

            <h3>5. Incident history matters</h3>

            <p>
              A resolved incident can still provide valuable information for
              future troubleshooting.
            </p>

            <h2>What I Would Improve Next</h2>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI is also a foundation for further development.
            </p>

            <p>Some areas I would explore next include:</p>

            <pre><code>More Kubernetes resource integrations
        ↓
Richer observability
        ↓
Better incident correlation
        ↓
More advanced AI investigation
        ↓
Safer automated remediation
        ↓
Improved incident history</code></pre>

            <p>
              I would also continue improving the separation between
              <strong>recommendation</strong> and
              <strong>automatic execution</strong> so that potentially
              disruptive infrastructure actions can be handled with appropriate
              controls.
            </p>

            <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

            <p>
              PlatformOps AI started with a simple question:
            </p>

            <blockquote>
              <strong>
                What if a DevOps engineer could see Kubernetes state,
                observability data and recent development activity in one place
                and use that information to investigate incidents more
                efficiently?
              </strong>
            </blockquote>

            <p>
              Building the platform gave me an opportunity to work across
              Kubernetes, infrastructure automation, observability, APIs,
              application development and AI-assisted operations.
            </p>

            <p>
              More importantly, it reinforced something I continue to learn
              through DevOps:
            </p>

            <blockquote>
              <strong>
                Infrastructure isn't just about keeping systems running. It's
                about understanding systems well enough to respond when they
                don't.
              </strong>
            </blockquote>

            <h2>Explore the Project</h2>

            <p>
              The complete implementation is available on GitHub:
            </p>

            <a
              class="project-link"
              href="https://github.com/feranzeey/platformops-ai"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              View PlatformOps AI on GitHub →
            </a>

            <p style="margin-top: 25px;">
              <strong>Technologies:</strong> Kubernetes · Terraform · Prometheus
              · Docker · Python · Flask · GitHub API · AI
            </p>

          </div>
        </article>
      </main>

      <footer class="footer">
        <div class="container">
          © {new Date().getFullYear()} Feranzeey. Built with Astro.
        </div>
      </footer>
